hypotheses.statement:
The fields $k$ and $l$ have the same characteristic. There is a field isomorphism $\sigma:k\longrightarrow l$ such that, for every good function $t$, there is $H_t\in\mathcal H_l$ with $$\label{eq:field-alignment}
 B_t=H_t\circ\sigma
 \qquad\text{outside a finite subset of }\mathbb P^1(k),$$ where $\sigma(\infty)=\infty$.

outputs.exact_statement:
Let $B/b$ be a function field of transcendence degree one over an algebraically closed field, and let $C/b$ be its smooth projective curve. For $x\in C(b)$, let $\delta_x=\mathop{\mathrm{ord}}_x\bmod\ell\in A_B$. The lines $\Lambda\delta_x$ are distinct and nonzero. The family $(\delta_x)_{x\in C(b)}$ tends to zero outside finite subsets, and the continuous summation map $$\label{eq:curve-summation}
 \prod_{x\in C(b)}\Lambda\longrightarrow A_B,\qquad
 (a_x)_x\longmapsto\sum_x a_x\delta_x$$ has kernel consisting exactly of the constant families. If $\mathop{\mathrm{char}}(b)\ne\ell$, the common kernel in $V_B$ of the $\delta_x$ has dimension $2g(C)$.

Let $X\subset\mathbb P_l^n$ be a normal integral projective variety of dimension $d\ge2$ over an algebraically closed field $l$ of characteristic different from $\ell$, and put $L=l(X)$. Let $\mathcal S$ be an infinite set of prime divisors on $X$ whose degrees are bounded. Suppose that $H\subset V_L$ is a subspace with the following property: for every $g\in L^\times$ with $[g]\in H$, $$\label{eq:curve-residue-trivial}
 \mathop{\mathrm{ord}}_D(g)=0\quad\hbox{and}\quad
 g|_D\in l(D)^{\times\ell}
 \qquad\hbox{for all but finitely many }D\in\mathcal S.$$ Then there are a finite extension $M/L$ and a one-variable field $P_0/l$ contained in $M$ such that $M/P_0$ is regular, $$\label{eq:curve-compositum}
                               M=LP_0,$$ and the image of $H$ in $V_M$ is contained in the image of $V_{P_0}$. In particular, every $g$ as above has an $\ell$-th root in $M\overline{P_0}$.

Let $t,s$ be algebraically independent good functions such that $z_c=t+cs$ is good for every $c\in k^\times$. For every $c\in k^\times$ there is a perfect rational function $R_c\in l(X,Y)^i$ such that $$\label{eq:field-sum}
 R_c(x,y)=B_{z_c}\bigl(B_t^{-1}(x)+cB_s^{-1}(y)\bigr)
 \qquad\text{for general }(x,y)\in l^2.$$ The one-variable slices of these functions give $$\label{eq:field-affine}
 B_sB_t^{-1}\in\mathcal H_l,
 \qquad
 B_t\mathop{\mathrm{Aff}}(k)B_t^{-1}\subset\mathcal H_l.$$ The second inclusion is an embedding of groups.

The fields $k$ and $l$ have the same characteristic. There is a field isomorphism $\sigma:k\longrightarrow l$ such that, for every good function $t$, there is $H_t\in\mathcal H_l$ with $$\label{eq:field-alignment}
 B_t=H_t\circ\sigma
 \qquad\text{outside a finite subset of }\mathbb P^1(k),$$ where $\sigma(\infty)=\infty$.
